\begin{abstract}
\noindent
 This paper uses the differential equation method to derive several new solutions to the Schrodinger equation, which has many applications in mathematical physics. This method effectively produces bell-shaped solitons, singular solitons, dark solitons, bright solitons, and some periodic solitons to solve non-linear partial differential equations. These types of solutions are more than those offered by current technologies. The parameters in the solution are to provide the necessary results for understanding the body and providing beautiful images. Our results demonstrate the effectiveness, efficiency, and desirability of the differential equation method in obtaining good results in solving many different differential equations.
\end{abstract}

\textbf{Key words}: Auxiliary equation method, Schrödinger equation, Analytical soliton solution, Dark soliton, Bright soliton, Singular soliton and Kink shaped solitons.
\section{Introduction}
\noindentThe study of solitons and their properties has become a cornerstone of modern mathematical physics particularly in the context of non-linear partial differential equations (PDEs). Among these, the Non-linear Schrödinger Equation (NLSE) stands out as one of the most important equations widely studied in the context of fluid dynamics, non-linear optics and plasma physics. Solitons as stable wave-like solutions of non-linear equations offer unique insights into the behavior of complex systems where wave propagation is governed by both dispersive and non-linear effects. Solitons are non-linear waves. A soliton is considered as solitory traveling wave pulse solution of non-linear partial differential equations. There are non-linear wave phenomena in several areas including blood physics.\cite{IFE3,A2,A3}, optical fiber \cite{a4,a5,a6,a7}, fluid dynamics \cite{a8,a9}, and other non-linear wave phenomena we can get by finding the exact solutions of the non-linear partial differential equations. In the study of non-linear phenomena, the solution to these NPDEs is crucial. Numerous techniques have been developed in recent years to solve NPDE's such as the $G/G^{'}$ - Expansion method \cite{a8}, Backscatter method \cite{a10}, The Exponential Function method \cite{a11},  Homotopy perturbation method \cite{a12}, Hirota bilinear method \cite{a13}, Homogeneous balance method \cite{a14}, Bäcklund transform \cite{a15}, and Jacobi and Weierstrass elliptic function method \cite{a16} etc.\\\
The non-linear Schrodinger equation (NLSE) has this form \cite{a17}:
\begin{eqnarray}\label{F}
i u_{t}+u_{\mathit{xx}}\pm 2 \gamma  {| u |}^{2} u = 0,
\end{eqnarray}
Where $\gamma\neq {0}$ and is a real constant and a complex-valued function of two real variables, x and t, is u = u (x,t). The Schrödinger equation is used in many areas of physics, including superconductivity, non-linear optics, quantum mechanics, and plasma physics \cite{a18}. Isolated solutions are indicated by the NLSE (1.1). These are referred to as NLS+ and NLS-equivalent circumstances. Non-linear Schrodinger positive and Non-linear Schrodinger negative have different overlaps. The NLS ''+'' and NLS ''-'' overlaps are not the same. For instance, NLS- has a dark resolution while NLS+ has a bright one resolution \cite{a19}. Such behavior occurs when there is no conflict between the two plasma instabilities \cite{a20} and the two layers of baroclinic instability \cite{a21}.\\
The UNLS equation takes the form:
\begin{eqnarray}\label{F}
\mathit{iu}_{t}+u_{\mathit{xx}}+2 \gamma  {| u |}^{2} u -2 \gamma  u =  0.
\end{eqnarray}
Vadati et al.\cite{a22} A similar Hamiltonian complex has been proposed as a modification of the amplitude equation (UNS).
\begin{eqnarray}\label{F}
\mathit{iu}_{t}+u_{\mathit{xx}}+2 \gamma  {| u |}^{2} u -\epsilon  u_{\mathit{xt}} =  0.
\end{eqnarray}
Identifies some instabilities of the modulated wave train \cite{a23}.\\\
The time-dependent, two-dimensional NLSE :
\begin{eqnarray}\label{F}
\mathit{iu}_{t}+u_{\mathit{xx}}+u_{\mathit{yy}}+\gamma  {| u |}^{2} u =  0.
\end{eqnarray}
This type of equation, which simulates an electromagnetic wave equation in a two-dimensional weakly guiding structure, is found in many areas of physics, including quantum mechanics \cite{a24}, Electromagnetic Wave Propagation \cite{a25}, Underwater Acoustics \cite{a26} and  and the design of specific optoelectronic devices \cite{a27} Because it simulates the electromagnetic wave balance in a two-dimensional weak conductor model. It is also used in many quantum dynamics calculations \cite{a28}.\\\
The time-dependent 3D NLSE non-linear schrodinger equation is expressed as:
\begin{eqnarray}\label{F}
i u_{t}+u_{\mathit{xx}}+u_{\mathit{yy}}+u_{\mathit{zz}}+\gamma  {| u |}^{2} u =  0.
\end{eqnarray}
Gagnon and Winternitz reported symmetry reductions of the (3 + 1)-dimensional non-linear Schrodinger equation in a number of publications \cite{a29,a30,a31,a32,a33}.

\section{The Simple Auxiliary Method }
\subsection{Steps of the Simple Auxiliary Method}
The followings are the steps involved in the Simple auxiliary equation method:\\\
\textbf{\textbf{$1^{st}$} Step:}
We use the transformation equation
to convert the (NLPDE) non-linear partial differential equation
\begin{eqnarray}
H(u,u_x,u_y,u_t,u_{xy},........)=0,
\end{eqnarray}
into (ODE) ordinary differential equation form (By uniting the independent variables we get).
\begin{eqnarray}\label{b1}
G(U,U',U'',U''',.............)=0.
\end{eqnarray}\\\
\textbf{\textbf{$2^{nd}$} Step:}
 We now examine the solution to the equation of the following form:
\begin{eqnarray}
U \! \left(\zeta \right)= a_{0} + a_{1} Q \! \left(\zeta \right)+ a_{2} {U \! \left(\zeta \right)}^{2} +........+a_{N} {Q \! \left(\zeta \right)}^{N}.
\end{eqnarray}
\textbf{\textbf{$3^{rd}$} Step:}
To obtain the value of N we now use the principles of homogeneous equilibrium and balance which takes into account the highest order derivative term and non-linear factor involved in the equation (2.2).\\\
The $Q{(\zeta)}$ shows the answers of the equation (2.4).
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{d}{d \zeta}Q \! \left(\zeta \right) =\sqrt{a Q \! \left(\xi \right)^{2}+b Q \! \left(\xi \right)^{3}+c Q \! \left(\xi \right)^{4}},
\end{eqnarray}
where a,b,and c are parameter (real valued).\\\\
\textbf{\textbf{$4^{th}$} Step:}
Substitute equation (2.4) and using the transformation in equation (2.2) we get a resulting algebraic equation in ${Q(\zeta)}$. Now we simplify our resulting equation to get the coefficients of all powers of ${Q(\zeta)}$ and then put all the coefficients of all powers of $Q{(\zeta)}$ equal to zero. This will create a system of equations that can be solved separately and simultaneously.\\\
The exact solutions of equation (2.4) using ${\epsilon=\pm1}$ are:\\\\
\textbf{Family I: for $a > 0$ }
\begin{eqnarray}
Q \! \left(\zeta \right) = -\frac{a b\,\mathrm{sech}\! \left(\frac{\sqrt{a}\, \xi}{2}\right)^{2}}{b^{2}-a c \left[1+\epsilon  \tanh \! \left(\frac{\sqrt{a}\, \xi}{2}\right)\right]^{2}}.
\end{eqnarray}
\textbf{Family II: for $a > 0$ }
\begin{eqnarray}
Q \! \left(\zeta \right) = \frac{a b\,\mathrm{csch}\! \left(\frac{\sqrt{a}\, \xi}{2}\right)^{2}}{b^{2}-a c \left[1+\epsilon  \coth \! \left(\frac{\sqrt{a}\, \xi}{2}\right)\right]^{2}}.
\end{eqnarray}
\textbf{Family III: for $a > 0$ , \,$b^{2}-4ac > 0$ }
\begin{eqnarray}
Q \! \left(\zeta \right) =\frac{2 a \,\mathrm{sech}\! \left(\sqrt{a}\, \xi \right)}{\epsilon  \sqrt{-4 a c +b^{2}}-b \,\mathrm{sech}\! \left(\sqrt{a}\, \xi \right)}.
\end{eqnarray}
\textbf{Family IV: for $a < 0$ , \,$b^{2}-4ac > 0$ }
\begin{eqnarray}
Q \! \left(\zeta \right) =\frac{2 a \sec \! \left(\sqrt{-a}\, \xi \right)}{\epsilon  \sqrt{-4 a c +b^{2}}-b \sec \! \left(\sqrt{-a}\, \xi \right)}.
\end{eqnarray}
\textbf{Family V: for $a > 0$ , \,$b^{2}-4ac < 0$ }
\begin{eqnarray}
Q \! \left(\zeta \right) =\frac{2 a \,\mathrm{csch}\! \left(\sqrt{a}\, \xi \right)}{\epsilon  \sqrt{4 a c -b^{2}}-b \,\mathrm{csch}\! \left(\sqrt{a}\, \xi \right)}.
\end{eqnarray}
\textbf{Family VI: for $a < 0$ , \,$b^{2}-4ac > 0$ }
\begin{eqnarray}
Q \! \left(\zeta \right) =\frac{2 a \csc \! \left(\sqrt{-a}\, \xi \right)}{\epsilon  \sqrt{-4 a c +b^{2}}-b \csc \! \left(\sqrt{-a}\, \xi \right)}.
\end{eqnarray}
\textbf{Family VII: for $a > 0$ , \,$c > 0$ }
\begin{eqnarray}
Q \! \left(\zeta \right) =-\frac{a \mathrm{sech}\! \left(\frac{\sqrt{a}\, \xi}{2}\right)^{2}}{b +2\,\epsilon  \sqrt{a c}\, \tanh \! \left(\frac{\sqrt{a}\, \xi}{2}\right)}.
\end{eqnarray}
\textbf{Family VIII: for $a < 0$ , \,$c > 0$ }
\begin{eqnarray}
Q \! \left(\zeta \right) =-\frac{a \left[\sec^{2}\left(\frac{\sqrt{-a}\, \xi}{2}\right)\right]}{b +2\,\epsilon  \sqrt{-a c}\, \tan \! \left(\frac{\sqrt{-a}\, \xi}{2}\right)}.
\end{eqnarray}
\textbf{Family IX: for $a > 0$ , \,$c > 0$   }
\begin{eqnarray}
Q \! \left(\zeta \right) =\frac{a \mathrm{csch}\! \left(\frac{\sqrt{a}\, \xi}{2}\right)^{2}}{b +2\,\epsilon  \sqrt{a c}\, \coth \! \left(\frac{\sqrt{a}\, \xi}{2}\right)}.
\end{eqnarray}
\textbf{Family X: for $a < 0$ , \,$c > 0$ }
\begin{eqnarray}
Q \! \left(\zeta \right) =-\frac{a \left[\csc^{2}\left(\frac{\sqrt{-a}\, \xi}{2}\right)\right]}{b +2\,\epsilon  \sqrt{-a c}\, \cot \! \left(\frac{\sqrt{-a}\, \xi}{2}\right)}.
\end{eqnarray}
\textbf{Family XI: for $a > 0$ , \,$b^{2}-4ac = 0$  }
\begin{eqnarray}
Q \! \left(\zeta \right) =-\frac{a \left[1+\epsilon  \tanh \! \left(\frac{\sqrt{a}\, \xi}{2}\right)\right]}{b}.
\end{eqnarray}
\textbf{Family XII: for  $a > 0$ , \,$b^{2}-4ac = 0$ }
\begin{eqnarray}
Q \! \left(\zeta \right) =-\frac{a \left[1+\epsilon  \coth \! \left(\frac{\sqrt{a}\, \xi}{2}\right)\right]}{b}.
\end{eqnarray}
\textbf{Family XIII: for $a > 0$ }
\begin{eqnarray}
Q \! \left(\zeta \right) =\frac{4 a \,{\mathrm e}^{\epsilon  \sqrt{a}\, \zeta}}{\left({\mathrm e}^{\epsilon  \sqrt{a}\, \zeta}-b \right)^{2}-4 a c}.
\end{eqnarray}
\textbf{Family XIV:  for $a > 0$ ,\, $b = 0$  }
\begin{eqnarray}
Q \! \left(\zeta \right) =\frac{\left(\pm 4\right) a \epsilon  \,{\mathrm e}^{\epsilon  \sqrt{a}\, \zeta}}{1-4 a c \,{\mathrm e}^{4 \epsilon  \sqrt{a}\, \zeta}}.
\end{eqnarray}
\section{Solution of Non-Linear Schrodinger Equation }
Consider NLSE with this form:
\begin{eqnarray}
i u_{t}+u_{\mathit{xx}}+2 \gamma  {| u |}^{2} u = 0,
\end{eqnarray}
Now we use this transformation equation
 \begin{eqnarray}
u \! \left(x , t\right) ={\mathrm e}^{i\theta} U \! \left(\zeta \right),   \qquad \zeta = x + v t,  \qquad \theta = p x + r t.
\end{eqnarray}
and using the relation $v=-2p$ we get
\begin{eqnarray}
U^{''}{(\zeta)}-\left(p^{2}+r \right) U \! \left(\zeta \right)+2 \gamma \! \left(U \! \left(\zeta \right)\right)^{3}=0.
\end{eqnarray}
the ODE form of the schrodinger equation. N= 1 is now obtained by balancing the highest degree non-linear term $U^{3}$ and the maximum order derivative term $U''$.\\\
Using $N=1$ in equation (2.4) will gives:
\begin{eqnarray}
U \! \left(\zeta \right)= a_{0} + a_{1} Q \! \left(\zeta \right),
\end{eqnarray}
Here we need to find the constants involved in $U{(\zeta)}$ which are $a_{0}$ and $a_{1}$.\\\
The $Q{(\zeta)}$ represents (the families given) the solutions of equation (2.4). Now by putting equation (3.2) and (3.4) in eq.(3.3). After this we simplify our resulting equation to get the coefficients of all powers of ${Q(\zeta)}$ and then put all the coefficients of all non zero powers of $Q{(\zeta)}$ equal to zero. This will create a set of algebraic equations. These equations can be solved separately or simultaneously.\\\
The non-linear algebraic equations are:\\\
\begin{align}
\begin{cases}
Q^{3}{(\zeta)}:\qquad2 \gamma  a_{1}^{3}+2 a_{1} c = 0,\\\
Q^{2}{(\zeta)}:\qquad\frac{3}{2} a_{1} b +6 \gamma  a_{0} a_{1}^{2} = 0,\\\
Q^{1}{(\zeta)}:\qquad6 \gamma  a_{0}^{2} a_{1}-a_{1} p^{2}+a_{1} a -a_{1} r = 0,\\\
Q^{0}{(\zeta)}:\qquad2 \gamma  a_{0}^{3}-a_{0} p^{2}-a_{0} r = 0.\\\
\end{cases}
\end{align}
By using Maple software we obtained the solution of these equations.\\\
\textbf{Set:}\\\
$a_{0} = -\frac{b}{4 \sqrt{-\gamma  c}}$, \,$a_{1} = \frac{\sqrt{-\gamma  c}}{\gamma}$ and  \,$\nu = - 5\times 10^{-1} \sqrt{-\frac{6 b^{2}}{c}+16 a -16 r}$.\\\
\textbf{ Solution of Set:}\\\
Using equation (3.2) and \textbf{family (1,2,11,12)} in equation (3.4) we get the following results:
\begin{eqnarray}
U_{1}\left(\zeta \right) =-\frac{b}{4 \sqrt{-\gamma  c}}-\frac{\sqrt{c (-\gamma)}\, a b\, \mathrm{sech} \left[\frac{\sqrt{a}\, \left(t v +x \right)}{2}\right]^{2}}{\gamma  \left[b^{2}-a c \left(1+\epsilon  \tanh  \left(\frac{\sqrt{a}\, \left(t v +x \right)}{2}\right)\right)^{2}\right]}.
\end{eqnarray}
\begin{eqnarray}
U_{2}\left(\zeta \right) =-\frac{b}{4 \sqrt{-\gamma  c}}+\frac{\sqrt{-\gamma  c}\, a b\, \mathrm{csch} \left[\frac{\sqrt{a}\, \left(t v +x \right)}{2}\right]^{2}}{\gamma  \left[b^{2}-a c \left(1+\epsilon  \coth  \left(\frac{\sqrt{a}\, \left(t v +x \right)}{2}\right)\right)^{2}\right]}.
\end{eqnarray}
\begin{eqnarray}
U_{3}\left(\zeta \right) =-\frac{b}{4 \sqrt{-\gamma  c}}-\frac{\sqrt{-\gamma  c}\, a \left[1+\epsilon  \tanh  \left(\frac{\sqrt{a}\, \left(t v +x \right)}{2}\right)\right]}{\gamma  b}.
\end{eqnarray}
\begin{eqnarray}
U_{4}\left(\zeta \right) =
-\frac{b}{4 \sqrt{-\gamma  c}}-\frac{\sqrt{-\gamma  c}\, a \left[1+\epsilon  \coth \left(\frac{\sqrt{a}\, \left(t v +x \right)}{2}\right)\right]}{\gamma  b}.
\end{eqnarray}
