\section{Results and Discussion}\label{s4}
\noindent This paper will present new solutions to the non-linear schrodinger equation then those we have obtained using alternative methods. To the best of our knowledge, several solutions using  auxiliary equation method for the Schrodinger problem are novel and have not been published in any literature. Proofs of analytic solutions for soliton waves, including hyperbolic functions, weakly acting functions, value functions, exponential functions and the triangular function solution problem of the Schrodinger equation were found. The following observations can be used to explain the physical result of these solutions with 3D and 2D Contour density graphs by utilizing regular measurements and the suitable values of unknown coefficients. Figure 1 shows the Dark soliton observed for (3.6). Figure 2 shows the Bright soliton  observed for (3.7). Furthermore some similar solitory waves are also observed. Figure 3 shows the Kink shaped soliton observed for (3.8). The presentation of the analytical soliton solutions and dynamic behavior involves use of the appropriate values for the free parameter constants and unknown coefficients.
\section{Conclusion}\label{s5}
\noindent This work provides numerous new accurate soliton solutions and suggests using the auxiliary equation approach to solve the Schrodinger problem. These new solutions may also help to clarify the physical properties of the given equation. Maple is used to verify the solutions that have obtained. This approach is found to be more broad, simple and intuitive which explains its efficacy, generative feature and suitability for handling a wide range of additional non-linear partial differential equations.
